    [CI] Add MRV2 context-parallel accuracy coverage for SFA, GQA and MLA (#18267)
    
    ### What this PR does / why we need it?
    
    Add A3-560T nightly accuracy coverage for MRV2 context parallelism
    across SFA, GQA, and MLA, including data parallelism. Reuse the existing
    YAML-driven single-node runner and AISBench assertions, with one
    combined scenario and one accuracy benchmark per configuration.
    
    | Nightly case | Model | DP / TP / PCP / DCP | Speculative decoding |
    Logical NPUs |
    | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
    | `GLM-5.2-W4A8C8-DSpark-MRV2-PCP` | GLM-5.2 W4A8C8 (SFA) | 2 / 4 / 2 /
    1 | DSpark, 7 tokens, eager draft | 16 |
    | `Qwen3-32B-QuaRot-eagle3-MRV2-PCP-DCP` | Qwen3-32B W8A8 QuaRot (GQA) |
    2 / 2 / 2 / 2 | Eagle3, 3 tokens | 8 |
    | `Kimi-K2.6-W4A8-MRV2-PCP-DCP` | Kimi-K2.6 W4A8 (MLA) | 2 / 4 / 2 / 8 |
    None | 16 |
    
    All cases enable `FULL_DECODE_ONLY`, chunked prefill, prefix caching,
    and async scheduling. GLM and Kimi enable expert parallelism. GLM uses
    an INT8 indexer cache and retains the default SFA KV-cache dtype. Kimi
    exercises `DCP = TP * PCP`; Qwen exercises `DCP = PCP`.
    
    Keep the configurations under
    `tests/e2e/cases/features/context_parallel/`, register them in the
    A3-560T nightly matrix with the full DP-inclusive resource count, and
    align the corresponding documentation tables and targeted invocation
    with that matrix. The MLA benchmark uses the existing Kimi weekly
    AIME2025 accuracy settings; GQA and GLM retain the corresponding
    existing GSM8K-lite accuracy settings. This PR changes three
    configuration files, nightly registration, and documentation.
    
    ### Does this PR introduce _any_ user-facing change?
    
    The three nightly cases can be selected with:
    
    ```text
    /nightly GLM-5.2-W4A8C8-DSpark-MRV2-PCP Qwen3-32B-QuaRot-eagle3-MRV2-PCP-DCP Kimi-K2.6-W4A8-MRV2-PCP-DCP --a3-560t
    ```
    
    There are no inference implementation, public API, or test-framework
    changes.
    
    ### How was this patch tested?
    
    Each submitted configuration matches its recorded runtime configuration
    after substituting existing local main/draft weights, dataset paths, and
    test labels. Each completed one valid accuracy round through the
    standard `tests/e2e/common/single_node/test_single_node.py` pytest entry
    point on Ascend A3, using Python 3.11.10, torch 2.10.0, torch_npu
    2.10.0.post7, and CANN 9.2.0.
    
    | Case | Dataset | Accuracy | Configured baseline / tolerance
    (percentage points) | pytest |
    | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
    | GLM / SFA | GSM8K-lite, 32 questions | 31/32, 96.875% (CSV: 96.88%) |
    95 / 5 | 1 passed |
    | Qwen / GQA | GSM8K-lite, 32 questions | 32/32, 100% | 100 / 2 | 1
    passed |
    | Kimi / MLA | AIME2025, 30 questions | 28/30, 93.33% | 96.67 / 10 | 1
    passed |
    
    These are configuration validation results on recorded experimental
    source snapshots, **not a hardware pass of this PR head**. The runs used
    vLLM HEAD `8cc9aa5ad350f6f80cf3bebb298d410803ff75a9` (working-tree
    snapshot `513c114a9435ea7d86ebfa35976d4c4acf3fc351`) and vllm-ascend
    HEAD `d6cc2cd6048d83f754823fcc82bf1f548ce7a774` with recorded
    uncommitted changes, including a PCP query-boundary fix (snapshot
    `f37deab83fa98008245b72e6fd2a2c23fddd6012`). They also used an
    experimental DP identity preservation patch in `pcp_utils.py`,
    `autoregressive/speculator.py`, and `eagle/speculator.py` (patch SHA256:
    `26717474155f0e95c2b6001e77c1077cc1b5d500776d1528f8f7c3286a9d0832`).
    Those runtime fixes are outside this configuration-only PR. Raw
    predictions, evaluator results, JUnit files, source identities, and
    cleanup evidence are retained in the local Run Manifests.
    
    **Before merging, validate the exact PR head with the CI-pinned vLLM
    revision and CI weights/dataset paths.** One round does not establish
    repeatability. The Kimi run took 99.59 minutes; its margin under the
    existing 120-minute CI timeout remains unverified. Configured reference
    scores are inherited benchmark settings, not newly measured non-CP
    baselines.
    
    CPU-only checks passed for the submitted diff: YAML/schema and
    DP/TP/PCP/DCP resource validation; semantic comparison with the recorded
    runtime configurations; the actual nightly matrix/dispatch resolver;
    preservation of all existing matrix entries; documentation names,
    grouping, and order; changed-file codespell, typos, markdownlint; and
    `git diff --check`.
    
    This naming update passed YAML parsing, a semantic comparison confirming
    that only three nightly registration names changed, documentation/matrix
    consistency checks, and the actual nightly matrix/dispatch resolver for
    each new name and their combined selection. Model case YAMLs, serving
    arguments, resource assignments, and accuracy gates are unchanged. No
    NPU test was started for this update.
    
    For a prepared A3 environment, run each configuration through the
    existing entry point (16 logical NPUs for GLM/Kimi, 8 for Qwen):
    
    ```bash
    CONFIG_BASE_PATH=tests/e2e/cases/features/context_parallel \
    CONFIG_YAML_PATH=GLM-5.2-W4A8C8-DSpark-MRV2-PCP.yaml \
    python -m pytest -s -v tests/e2e/common/single_node/test_single_node.py
    ```
    
    Repeat with `Qwen3-32B-QuaRot-eagle3-MRV2-PCP-DCP.yaml` and
    `Kimi-K2.6-W4A8-MRV2-PCP-DCP.yaml`. Full `format.sh ci`, CI
    downloads/cache paths, repeated fresh starts, eager/graph comparisons,
    full GSM8K, performance, dedicated long-input workloads, and
    prefix-cache-hit validation have not been run for this PR head.
    
    ---
    
    ### Checklist
    
    #### Testing
    
    - [ ] **Boundary / edge-case coverage**: I reviewed whether this change
    involves boundary conditions (e.g. empty input, single/zero element,
    min/max values, off-by-one, sequence-length limits, integer overflow).
    Where applicable, I added unit tests covering these edge cases in
    `tests/ut/` or `tests/e2e/`.
    - [ ] **Regression test**: If this PR fixes a bug, I added a regression
    test that would fail without the fix.
    - [ ] **Error / failure path coverage**: I covered abnormal paths
    (exceptions, timeouts, out-of-range, division by zero) in addition to
    the happy path.
    - [x] **Meaningful & reliable tests**: New tests assert real behavior
    and are not written merely to satisfy CI.
    - [ ] **Performance impact**: If this PR touches a performance-sensitive
    path, I attached benchmark results and added performance regression
    tests.
    
    
